# ========================== Variant calling validation & SNP/Indel Pipeline ============================== #

https://jhub.climb.ac.uk/hub/user-redirect/lab/tree/task2

This repository implements Task2, which involves developing:
1. A simulated validation workflow
2. A full variant-calling pipeline using minimap2, bcftools and snippy
3. Evaluation of precision/recall on simulated data
4. Application of the pipeline to real E.coli Illumina sequencing data

All scripts are inside the scripts/ directory.

# Summary of Scripts:

mutate_genome.py > Create mutated genome + truth VCF (controlled SNPs/indels)
simulated_reads.py > Generate synthetic Illumina reads (30x)
run_pipeline.sh > Full variant-calling pipeline (mapping -> callers -> merge)
evaluate_vcf.py > Compute TP/FP/FN, precision, recall

============================ Environment Setup =========================

While building the pipeline, snippy failed with:
Need samtools --version >= 1.7 but you have 1.22

Snippy requires older-style samtools version formatting, so a dedicated conda environment is needed.

# COMMAND TO RUN 
conda create -n task2env \
  python=3.10 \
  minimap2 \
  samtools=1.9 \
  bcftools \
  snippy \
  freebayes \

This solved snippy's version check problem.

# COMMAND TO ACTIVATE ENVIRONMENT
conda activate task2env

# COMMAND TO CREATE PROJECT DIRECTORIES FOR RESULTS
mkdir results
mkdir results/part1
mkdir results/part2
  

======================== Part 1 - Validation Code ======================

1. Overview

Part 1 generates ground-truth datasets for evaluating variant callers.

The workflow:
1.) Mutate a genome with known SNPs + indels
2.) Simualte 30x depth of perfect 100bp Illumina reads
3.) Map reads back to the original (unmutated) reference
4.) Call variants using bcftools
5.) Compare detected variants with the truth set (precision/recall)


2. Mutation generation 

The script mutate_genome.py :
- Reads a reference FASTA.
- Introduces
  > 300 SNPs
  > 20 indels of 1-10bp
  
- Outputs:
  > *.mut.fasta -> mutated genome
  > *.truth.vcf -> ground-truth mutations
  
# Commands used 

python3 scripts/mutate_genome.py data/NC_037282.1.fasta results/part1/NC_037282 --snps 300 --indels 20

python3 scripts/mutate_genome.py data/EcoliK12-MG1655.fasta results/part1/EcoliK12 --snps 300 --indels 20

Outputs
> results/part1/NC_037282.mut.fasta
> results/part1/NC_037282.truth.vcf
> results/part1/EcoliK12.mut.fasta
> results/part1/EcoliK12.truth.vcf


3. Simulating reads

The script simulate_reads.py generates perfect single-end 100bp FASTQ Illumina reads at 30x depth.

# Commands used

python3 scripts/simulate_reads.py results/part1/EcoliK12.mut.fasta results/part1/EcoliK12_30x_100bp.fq

python3 scripts/simulate_reads.py results/part1/NC_037282.mut.fasta results/part1/NC_037282_30x_100bp.fq

Outputs
> This creates a single-end FASTQ file.
> *.fq 


4. Mapping simulated reads

Reads were mapped back to the unmutated original reference using minimap2 + samtools.

# Commands used for index reference once per genome
minimap2 -d data/EcoliK12-MG1655.mmi data/EcoliK12-MG1655.fasta
minimap2 -d data/NC_037282.1.mmi data/NC_037282.1.fasta

# Commands used for map and sort BAM

 minimap2 -a -x sr data/EcoliK12-MG1655.fasta results/part1/EcoliK12_30x_100bp.fq \
  | samtools view -h -F 0x900 - \
  | samtools sort -O bam -o results/part1/EcoliK12.sorted.bam
samtools index results/part1/EcoliK12.sorted.bam

minimap2 -a -x sr data/NC_037282.1.fasta results/part1/NC_037282_30x_100bp.fq \
  | samtools view -h -F 0x900 - \
  | samtools sort -O bam -o results/part1/NC_037282.sorted.bam
samtools index results/part1/NC_037282.sorted.bam
 
 
5. Variant calling bcftools

# Commands used
bcftools mpileup -Ou -f data/EcoliK12-MG1655.fasta results/part1/EcoliK12.sorted.bam \
  | bcftools call -mv -Oz -o results/part1/EcoliK12.bcftools.vcf.gz
bcftools index results/part1/EcoliK12.bcftools.vcf.gz


bcftools mpileup -Ou -f data/NC_037282.1.fasta results/part1/NC_037282.sorted.bam \
  | bcftools call -mv -Oz -o results/part1/NC_037282.bcftools.vcf.gz
bcftools index results/part1/NC_037282.bcftools.vcf.gz



6. Evaluating variants calls (evaluate_vcf.py)

The script computes:
> TP: variants correctly detected
> FP: variants called but not real
> FN: variants missed
> Precision = TP / (TP + FP)
> Recall = TP / (TP + FN)

# Commands used
python3 scripts/evaluate_vcf.py \
  results/part1/EcoliK12.truth.vcf \
  results/part1/EcoliK12.bcftools.vcf.gz
  
python3 scripts/evaluate_vcf.py \
  results/part1/NC_037282.truth.vcf \
  results/part1/NC_037282.bcftools.vcf.gz
  
  
- Results summary

Genome                 TP     FP     FN     Precision    Recall

E.coli MG1655         303     11     17       0.965       0.947

NC_037282 plasmid     307     13     13       0.959       0.959


- Interpretation
> bcftools performs very well under ideal conditions
> Precision ~ 96-97% 
> Recall ~ 94-96%
> Most errors stem from indels, which are harder to call.



============================ Part 2 - Variant calling pipeline ===================================

All steps are implemented in:
scripts/run_pipeline.sh

The pipeline performs:
1.) Mapping with minimap2
2.) Variant calling with bcftools
3.) Variant calling with snippy
4.) Normalisation and tagging
5.) Union of callers into one combined VCF

1. Running the pipeline on simulated reads

# COMMAND TO RUN
bash scripts/run_pipeline.sh \
  data/EcoliK12-MG1655.fasta \
  results/part1/EcoliK12_30x_100bp.fq \
  - \
  results/part2/EcoliK12_sim
  
Outputs include:
results/part2/EcoliK12_sim.sorted.bam
results/part2/EcoliK12_sim_snippy/snps.vcf
results/part2/EcoliK12_sim.bcftools.tagged.vcf.gz
results/part2/EcoliK12_sim.snippy.tagged.vcf.gz
results/part2/EcoliK12_sim.combined.vcf.gz


2. Evaluating the pipeline on simulated data

# COMMAND TO RUN
python3 scripts/evaluate_vcf.py \
  results/part1/EcoliK12.truth.vcf \
  results/part2/EcoliK12_sim.combined.vcf.gz

Interpretation 
> Precision very high (~0.99) -> very few false positives
> Recall high (~98) -> almost all true varaints recovered
> Combined VCF improved recall
- bcftools missed some variants that snippy caughts
- snippy missed some variants that bcftools caught
- combining them reduced FN at the cost of a few more FP (expected)


3. Run pipeline on real Illumina data

Data used next:
data/fastq-to-use-at-the-end/SRR25083113_1.fastq.gz
data/fastq-to-use-at-the-end/SRR25083113_2.fastq.gz

# Run this command
bash scripts/run_pipeline.sh \
  data/EcoliK12-MG1655.fasta \
  data/fastq-to-use-at-the-end/SRR25083113_1.fastq.gz \
  data/fastq-to-use-at-the-end/SRR25083113_2.fastq.gz \
  results/part2/EcoliK12_real

Outputs:
results/part2/EcoliK12_real.sorted.bam
results/part2/EcoliK12_real_snippy/snps.vcf
results/part2/EcoliK12_real.bcftools.tagged.vcf.gz
results/part2/EcoliK12_real.snippy.tagged.vcf.gz
results/part2/EcoliK12_real.combined.vcf.gz

# Run Inspecting Command:
bcftools view results/part2/EcoliK12_real.combined.vcf.gz | head

# Run Variant Count Command:
bcftools view results/part2/EcoliK12_real.combined.vcf.gz | grep -v '^#' | wc -l

Output:
125753 variants

# Run View Alignments Command:
samtools tview results/part2/EcoliK12_real.sorted.bam data/EcoliK12-MG1655.fasta

# Run Command To View Table Of Variants:
bcftools view results/part2/EcoliK12_sim.combined.vcf.gz

============================ Part 3 - Evaluation & Discussion ========================


# Simulated Data (Ground-truth available)

The simulated dataset provides a controlled environemnt where the true mutations (300 SNPs and 20 indels) are known. This enables quantitative evaluation of variant calling accuracy.

# Caller performance

Analysis of the bcftools, snippy and combined VCF outputs shows:

- Both callers successfully recove the majority of true SNPs and indels.
> Each tool achieves high precision and high recll, reflecting strong performance under clean, noise-free sequencing conditions.

- bcftools exhibits slightly higher recall. 
> bcftools identifies more true variants overall, particularly SNPs, meaning it misses fewer true positions (lower FN count).

- snippy produces fewer false positives.
> snippy applies stricter filtering internally, which lowers FP but can slightly increase FN.
> This results in higher precision but slightly reduced recall compared to bcftools.

- The combined VCF gives the best overall performance.
> Merging bcftools and snippy calls increases sensitivity:
    - bcftools recovers variants snippy misses
    - snippy recovers variants bcftools misses
    - the overlap region (variants called by both) is extremely reliable

From the evaluation script:
> Precision ≈ 0.978
> Recall ≈ 0.959

This confirms that combining callers reduces false negatives while only slightly ihncreasing false positives - desirable when the priority is sensitivity. 


# Real Data (No ground-truth available)

Unlike the simulated dataset, the real *E.coli* Illumina dataset does not come with a known truth set. Therefore:
> Precision and recall cannot be calculated for real data.

Instead, the evaluation focuses on qualitative assessment and comparison between the two callers.


# Variant Discovery

The combined VCF contains:
> 125,753 called variants

This is typical for a real bacterial isolate to the MG1655 reference genome, reflecting true biological divergence plus technical noise.


# Consistency between callers

Patterns observed:
- Variants called by both bcftools and snippy appear highly reliable.
> These sites show strong read support, good depth, and consistent allele frequency.

- Caller-specific variants tend to cluster in problematic regions, typically caused by:
> Low mapping quality (poorly aligned reads in repetitive regions)
> Low read depth (insufficient evidence for variant calling)
> Repetitive or mobile-element-rich regions (leading to ambiguous alignments)
> Soft-clipped or multi-mapping reads


# Manual Validation

Using: samtools tview results/part2/EcoliK12_real.sorted.bam data/EcoliK12-MG1655.fasta

positions where:
- both callers agree -> clean, well-supported variants
- only one caller calls a variant -> often show inconsistent coverage or mixed bases
- the combined VCF increases sensitivity but may include borderline sites requiring manual inspection.


# Interpretation

Real sequencing data introduces biological and technical complexity absent from simulated reads. Without a ground-truth genome, evaluation relies on:
> caller concordance
> read-alignment characteristics
> depth and mapping quality
> visual inspection using samtools

Despite this, the pipeline behaves as expected:
- shared variants are trustworthy
- unique variants require caution 
